# BEGIN fork-flow:gitattributes — managed block; re-run the kit installer to update.
# Add your OWN merge=ours entries ABOVE this block (outside the markers): updates
# REPLACE everything between the markers, but any active line above is preserved.
#
# merge=ours overrides — LAST RESORT (see your fork memory AGENTS.md, Fork Maintenance).
#
# A path listed here keeps YOUR version on every upstream merge and silently
# hides upstream's changes. Use it only for whole files you intentionally own
# (branding, generated config). Audit every listed path on each merge, and
# never use it for one-line edits inside active upstream files — let those
# conflict so rerere can learn the fix.
#
# Requires:  git config merge.ours.driver true   (set during setup)
# Backstops: ./.fork/brief.sh flags changed merge=ours paths over the range;
#            ./.fork/verify.sh fails if a listed path matches no tracked file.
# Globs work (e.g. "*.svg", "assets/**") — both backstops read paths through git.
#
# Uncomment and adapt when you actually need one:
# src/branding/logo.svg     merge=ours
# src/config/branding.ts    merge=ours

# --- Syntax-aware merge via mergiraf (https://mergiraf.org) — ON by default ----
# A conservative AST merge driver that clears NOISE conflicts (reordered methods,
# import churn, formatting) and FALLS BACK to conflict markers when unsure, so it
# never silently mis-merges. The driver lines below are ACTIVE; they take effect
# once the per-clone driver is registered: run ./.fork/setup.sh (also applied by
# the kit's install.sh --setup). With the binary:
#   git config merge.mergiraf.driver 'mergiraf merge --git %O %A %B -s %S -x %X -y %Y -p %P -l %L'
# Without it, setup registers a safe Git text-merge fallback. Install Mergiraf with
# cargo install mergiraf / brew install mergiraf, then re-run setup to activate it.
# Add/trim the languages your fork edits; full list: mergiraf languages --gitattributes
# Do NOT route lockfiles or generated files through it — regenerate those instead
# (the unset lines below pull the lockfiles the globs would capture back out; the
# lockfile/REGEN recipe, not a structural merge, owns them). NOTE: Vue SFCs are
# NOT supported by mergiraf — plain text merge applies to *.vue.
*.rs    merge=mergiraf
*.ts    merge=mergiraf
*.tsx   merge=mergiraf
*.js    merge=mergiraf
*.jsx   merge=mergiraf
*.mjs   merge=mergiraf
*.cjs   merge=mergiraf
*.py    merge=mergiraf
*.go    merge=mergiraf
*.java  merge=mergiraf
*.rb    merge=mergiraf
*.kt    merge=mergiraf
*.kts   merge=mergiraf
*.php   merge=mergiraf
*.cs    merge=mergiraf
*.dart  merge=mergiraf
*.c     merge=mergiraf
*.h     merge=mergiraf
*.cc    merge=mergiraf
*.hh    merge=mergiraf
*.cpp   merge=mergiraf
*.hpp   merge=mergiraf
*.ex    merge=mergiraf
*.exs   merge=mergiraf
*.scala merge=mergiraf
*.sol   merge=mergiraf
# Declarative formats mergiraf merges structurally:
*.json  merge=mergiraf
*.yml   merge=mergiraf
*.yaml  merge=mergiraf
*.toml  merge=mergiraf
*.html  merge=mergiraf
*.xml   merge=mergiraf
*.ini   merge=mergiraf
*.properties merge=mergiraf
# Lockfiles the globs above would capture — regenerated, never merged:
package-lock.json   !merge
npm-shrinkwrap.json !merge
pnpm-lock.yaml      !merge
# END fork-flow:gitattributes
